#
# The disk pack program at boot: the card's two partitions mounted, and
# cadr-disk-packs serving the CADR's disks from the drive bay at /mnt/packs.
#
# **THE CARD IS WRITTEN ONCE AND NEVER LEAVES THE BOARD.**  That is what the
# second partition is for: the first holds the loader and the boot files and
# is not touched at run time, the second holds nothing but disk packs and is
# where a pack is copied to, renamed out of, or write-protected while the
# machine runs.  Both are FAT32 --- deliberately, so that the card can be
# read and written from Windows, which cannot write ext4 --- and the price is
# no journal, which is why the partition that would hurt to lose is the one
# that is mounted read-only.
#
#   /dev/mmcblk0p1 -> /mnt/card    READ-ONLY.  BOOT.BIN, u-boot.img and
#                                  uEnv.txt at the root, whose names are
#                                  fixed, and cadr.bit, the tree, the kernel
#                                  and the root filesystem in a folder named
#                                  for the board, which is what the TFTP
#                                  server's directory for it is called too.
#                                  A power cut while this
#                                  is writable is a board that needs a card
#                                  reader again, and this project's whole
#                                  point is that it does not.  To replace a
#                                  boot file from the board:
#                                      mount -o remount,rw /mnt/card
#                                      ... cp ...
#                                      sync && mount -o remount,ro /mnt/card
#   /dev/mmcblk0p2 -> /mnt/packs   READ-WRITE.  disk-pack-0.img to
#                                  disk-pack-7.img, whichever exist.  A pack
#                                  is the one thing on the card the machine
#                                  writes, and it is written in place --- a
#                                  pack has to stay the size the drive is, so
#                                  there is no temporary file and no rename,
#                                  as muir's diskpack says of its own writes.
#                                  The program syncs every block it writes.
#
# Neither the boot ROM nor U-Boot ever looks at the second partition.
#
# A card without either partition is not a fault here: the program starts
# anyway, finds an empty bay, says so, and goes on watching --- and the CADR
# sees a controller with no drive on any of the eight units until a pack is
# copied in, at which point that unit's drive comes ready with no restart.
#
# The log goes to the console, which is where the board is watched from
# (docs/boot.md).  start-stop-daemon's -b would send it to /dev/null, so the
# program is told where to write instead.
#
# **AND THE BOOT BUTTON'S STEP LIVES HERE, WHICH NEEDS SAYING.**  `fpgarc`'s
# `--no-auto-boot`, and SW0 on the board, each leave the CADR's boot button
# unpressed, as a CADR is when the power comes on, and the machine is held with
# RUN clear until somebody presses it.  Two things fix where that step goes.  It must run BEFORE this
# script starts the pack program, because the drive coming present is what
# lets the boot PROM go on and read a band.  And it must read `fpgarc`, which
# is on the partition this script is the one thing that mounts.  A step of its
# own at S79 would therefore have to mount that partition itself, leaving the
# mount with two owners and one unmounter, so the step is here, between the
# mount and the drive.  `hold_the_machine` below is the whole of it, and it
# prefixes its one line `cadr-boot:` rather than `cadr-disk-packs:` because it
# is the boot button's and not the pack program's.
#
# **AND THE DEBUG CABLE'S ROLE AND ITS WIRING ARE HERE FOR THE SAME TWO
# REASONS.**
# `--debug-cable-connect` asks this board to be the debugger on Pmod JA, and it
# reaches the fabric through `cadr-console` exactly as the hold does.  It is
# read from `fpgarc`, which is on the partition this script mounts, and a step
# of its own would have to know that this one had mounted it.  It runs AFTER
# the hold and before the drive, which is an order and not an accident: a board
# that is going to debug somebody else should have taken the connector before
# its own machine starts asking a drive for a band.  `connect_the_debug_cable`
# below is the whole of it, and it prefixes its lines `cadr-debug-cable:`
# rather than `cadr-disk-packs:` for `hold_the_machine`'s reason.
#
# **THE WIRING IS SET BEFORE THE ROLE AND NOT AFTER**, which is an order and
# not a tidiness: the fabric refuses a wiring that moves under a board that is
# already the debugger, so a line that came second would be dropped and the
# card's setting would never reach the connector.

PROG=/usr/bin/cadr-disk-packs
BOOT_DEV=/dev/mmcblk0p1
PACKS_DEV=/dev/mmcblk0p2
BOOT=/mnt/card
PACKS=/mnt/packs
PIDFILE=/var/run/cadr-disk-packs.pid

# The card's one file of flags, and cadr-common's reader.  `fpgarc` configures
# the CADR in the fabric the way `muirrc` beside it configures the CADR inside
# muir, and several programs take their own flags out of it, so the file goes
# through a filter and each program is handed the lines of the flags it owns.
# docs/fpgarc.md has every program's list; the reader's header is the
# contract.  This script owns exactly one flag.
RC="$PACKS/fpgarc"
FPGARC_SH=/usr/share/cadr/fpgarc.sh
. "$FPGARC_SH"

# And the daemon starter, cadr-common's too: it starts the program, looks for
# it a moment later, and prints what it says when it is not there.  Its header
# is the whole account, and the short of it is that `start-stop-daemon -b`
# sends a refusal to /dev/null, so a flag this program will not take would
# otherwise be a boot that printed OK.
DAEMON_SH=/usr/share/cadr/daemon.sh
. "$DAEMON_SH"

# **THE HOLD MARKER.**  It stands while the machine is held, and it is what
# `cadr-console` reads to refuse `start` and `step` with muir's own words and
# to remove when `boot` presses the button.  /var/run is on the root
# filesystem, which is a RAM disk unpacked at every boot, so a marker cannot
# in fact survive a reset --- it is removed below all the same, because a
# `restart` of this script is the case that can leave one standing and a
# marker that outlives its hold is a lie about the machine.
HELD=/var/run/cadr-held

start() {
	mkdir -p "$BOOT" "$PACKS"
	if mountpoint -q "$BOOT" 2>/dev/null || mount -t vfat -o ro "$BOOT_DEV" "$BOOT" 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: the boot partition is at $BOOT, read-only"
	else
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: no boot partition at $BOOT_DEV"
	fi
	if mountpoint -q "$PACKS" 2>/dev/null || mount -t vfat -o rw "$PACKS_DEV" "$PACKS" 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: the drive bay is at $PACKS"
	else
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: no pack partition at $PACKS_DEV; the bay will be empty"
	fi
	hold_the_machine
	connect_the_debug_cable
	cadr_daemon "$PROG" "$PIDFILE" --packs "$PACKS" --log /dev/console
}

# **LEAVE THE BOOT BUTTON UNPRESSED, AND THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO ASK FOR IT.**
# muir's `--no-auto-boot` leaves a CADR as it is when the power comes on: RUN
# clear, nothing run, and only the button starts it.  On this board that is
# asked for by the card's flag or by the switch, and the two are an OR --- a
# flag can never turn a switch off.
#
#   SW0 on the board     THE FABRIC has already done it.  The machine came out
#                        of reset with RUN clear and has never run a
#                        microcycle, so there is nothing here to halt; what
#                        this step does is leave the marker, so that
#                        cadr-console refuses start and step and says why.
#                        `cadr-console switch` exits 0 when the switch held it.
#   --no-auto-boot in    NOTHING in the fabric changes for it, so the machine
#   fpgarc               is held by halting it, and this is the last moment
#                        before the drive comes present, which is what a held
#                        machine must not see.
#
# **THE PROM HAS ALREADY RUN WHEN THE FLAG IS WHAT HOLDS IT, AND THAT IS NOT A
# GAP IN THE HOLD.**  The CADR starts when the bitstream is loaded, seconds
# before Linux reaches this script, and within a few hundred milliseconds it
# has cleared its control store and is in AWAIT-DRIVE-READY.  It can go no
# further, because no drive is present until the program below starts.  So the
# machine this halts is one that has done its PROM work and is waiting, with
# nothing of a band loaded; `cadr-console boot` presses the button and the PROM
# starts again from zero.  **The switch has no such gap**: the machine never
# ran at all.
#
# Nothing is printed when neither asks for it, because a board that boots
# itself has nothing to say about it.
hold_the_machine() {
	rm -f "$HELD"
	# The card's flag.  A card with no fpgarc has no flag.
	no_auto_boot_flag=no
	if [ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --no-auto-boot; then
		no_auto_boot_flag=yes
	fi
	# And the switch, asked of the fabric itself rather than of any file.
	# A console that cannot be reached answers no, which is the safe
	# direction: it is also the console that could not have halted anything.
	no_auto_boot_switch=no
	if command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1 &&
	   cadr-console switch > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		no_auto_boot_switch=yes
	fi
	[ "$no_auto_boot_flag" = yes ] || [ "$no_auto_boot_switch" = yes ] || return 0

	if [ "$no_auto_boot_switch" = yes ]; then
		# Already held, by the fabric, since the reset.  No halt is
		# issued: there is nothing running to halt, and a halt here
		# would be this script taking credit for the switch's work.
		echo "held at boot by SW0" > "$HELD"
		if [ "$no_auto_boot_flag" = yes ]; then
			echo "cadr-boot: SW0: the machine came up with RUN clear and has never run;" \
			     "--no-auto-boot in $RC asks for the same thing"
		else
			echo "cadr-boot: SW0: the machine came up with RUN clear and has never run"
		fi
	else
		if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot, and no cadr-console to halt the machine with;" \
			     "it is running"
			return 0
		fi
		if ! cadr-console halt > /dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot, and cadr-console could not halt the machine;" \
			     "it is running"
			return 0
		fi
		echo "held at boot by --no-auto-boot in $RC" > "$HELD"
		echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot: the machine is held with RUN clear"
	fi
	echo "cadr-boot: \`cadr-console boot\` or BTN0 on the board presses the boot button"
}

# **WHICH WAY ROUND THE JA RIBBON WAS MADE.**  A Pmod cable is supposed to join
# pin one to pin one.  One made from two host sockets mirrors the header's two
# rows instead, so each board's pins 1 to 4 reach the other's 7 to 10, and a
# debugger then drives four pins the far board never listens to.  Two boards
# were found on exactly such a cable.
#
# `auto` is the fabric's own default and looks for the answer, so a card that
# says nothing is a board that finds out for itself; `straight` and `crossover`
# take the looking out of the way when somebody is diagnosing a cable.  Only a
# DEBUGGER applies the setting, so it changes nothing on a board that is a
# debuggee --- but it is set anyway and before the role, because the fabric
# refuses a wiring that moves under a board that already has the role.
#
# Nothing is printed when the flag is not there: a board on `auto` has nothing
# to say about it until it takes the role.
set_the_debug_cable_wiring() {
	[ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-wiring || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-wiring, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "the fabric's own default stands"
		return 0
	fi
	# One flag, one value, and the reader hands back both words.  A spelling
	# the console does not know is refused BY the console, which prints the
	# three it takes.
	eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --debug-cable-wiring)"
	if ! cadr-console debug-cable-wiring "${2:-auto}" 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-wiring ${2:-auto}: cadr-console could not" \
		     "reach the console; the fabric's own default stands"
	fi
}

# **THE DEBUG CABLE'S ROLE ON PMOD JA.**  A board with nothing said is a
# DEBUGGEE: it answers a debugger that plugs into the connector, exactly as
# MIT's board answers one on its DBGIN, and nothing has to be set for it.
# `--debug-cable-connect` asks for the other role, and it is muir's own flag
# with muir's own meaning.  There is no listen flag here or there, because
# listening is what a CADR always does.
#
# **ASKING IS NOT HAVING, so the line says what happened and not what was
# asked.**  A board that can see a debugger already driving the connector
# refuses, and the first board told is the one that has it.  `cadr-console
# debug-cable-connect` reads the role back after the write and prints it, so
# what reaches the console at boot is the outcome.
#
# Nothing is printed when the flag is not there, because a board that is a
# debuggee has nothing to say about it.
connect_the_debug_cable() {
	set_the_debug_cable_wiring
	[ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-connect || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-connect, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "this board is still a debuggee"
		return 0
	fi
	if ! cadr-console debug-cable-connect 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-connect: cadr-console could not reach the" \
		     "console; this board is still a debuggee"
	fi
}

stop() {
	printf "Stopping cadr-disk-packs: "
	start-stop-daemon -K -q -p "$PIDFILE"
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
	sync
	umount "$PACKS" 2>/dev/null
	umount "$BOOT" 2>/dev/null
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
